                <pb facs="0197" n="193" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg193"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/14257: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Linnell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-10-31T17:00:00+08:00">October 31, 1938—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 31—12:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>37. Reference is made to my two telegrams numbers 34, October 30;<anchor n="54"
                        xml:id="d233-f1"/> and 35, today.<anchor n="55" xml:id="d233-f2"/> The
                    British Consul General has now informed me that the Chinese Army have stopped
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">Robin</hi> at Shinling on the West River and warned
                    it that there are mines in the river between there and Howlik about 7 miles
                    above Samshui. Also an officer from the Japanese headquarters called today on
                    the British Consul General and stated that joint military and naval headquarters
                    at Tokyo had sent instructions that river was closed irrevocably and that no
                    vessels could pass at Samshui and referred further to the general notification
                    of October 22. See Gauss’ October 22, 5 p.m.</p>
                <p>Information sent to Chungking, Peiping and Hong Kong.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Linnell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
